// SOS M5 arena unit: A PACKAGE THAT CHOOSES ITS OWN ARENA (sawos design 38).
//
// **THE MECHANISM'S ONE PROOF, AND IT IS A PAIR OF BRACKETS.** `sosrt`'s bump
// arena used to be a `static` in the SHARED runtime module, so its 64 KiB was
// one compile-time fact for the kernel and for every process image in the
// system at once. It is a LINKER REGION now, and this package's manifest names
// a two-line script (`arena-small.riscv32.ld`) that sets `ARENA_SIZE = 8K`
// before INCLUDEing the same `root.ld` every other root package names.
//
// Nothing in this file mentions any of that — there is no API to ask an arena
// how big it is, and there should not be, since a program that could ask would
// be a program that could branch on it. What the program does instead is make
// two requests whose answers, together, pin the size to a range only the
// override can produce:
//
//   SOS arenasmall: 2 KiB ok          <- the region is REAL (a 0 KiB arena, or
//                                        one the linker forgot, fails here)
//   SOS arenasmall: asking 16 KiB     <- printed BEFORE the ask, because the
//                                        ask does not return
//   sos: out of arena memory          <- `sosrt`'s own honest failure, and the
//                                        machine stops with `AbortCode.NoMemory`
//
// **THE SECOND BRACKET IS THE ONE THAT PROVES THE OVERRIDE.** 16 KiB is past
// this package's 8 KiB and far inside the 64 KiB default, so an image that did
// NOT take the override serves the request, runs on, and prints the
// UNREACHABLE line at the bottom — which fails the case loudly rather than
// passing by omission. The first bracket rules out the other way to be wrong: a
// build where the region ended up empty or unreserved would fail at 2 KiB, and
// "everything fails" is not evidence that a size was chosen.
//
// **THE FAILURE IS AN ABORT, NOT A STATUS, AND THAT IS THE RUNTIME'S RULE
// RATHER THAN THIS UNIT'S.** `__saw_rt_alloc` has nowhere to report to — it IS
// the allocator every reporting path would allocate through — so it writes one
// line and stops the machine (`rt/common/src/lib.saw`). The `catch` arms below
// are therefore unreachable on this runtime and are written anyway, because the
// signatures carry an error channel and a bind-or-bail arm is what this tree
// writes at one.
//
// It runs on ALL THREE PROFILES. Nothing here is a hardware denial — a link
// address and a bump cursor are the same on a flat platform as on an isolated
// one — so the flat tier witnesses the mechanism exactly as the other two do.

import sos.{System}

/// 512 words = 2 KiB. Comfortably inside the 8 KiB the script asks for, with
/// room for whatever the allocator rounds or aligns.
static WITHIN_WORDS: Int = 512

/// 4096 words = 16 KiB. Both bounds are load-bearing: past this package's
/// 8 KiB, and far inside the 64 KiB every package that overrides nothing gets.
static BEYOND_WORDS: Int = 4096

/// A pattern with bits in both nibbles of every byte, so reading it back is a
/// claim about the storage rather than a coincidence with zeroed `.bss`.
static WITNESS: UInt32 = 0xA5A5_5A5A

static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    // BRACKET ONE. A request the override must still be able to serve.
    var within = try Vector<UInt32>(capacity: WITHIN_WORDS) catch {
        print("SOS arenasmall: 2 KiB refused: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try within.push(WITNESS) catch {
        print("SOS arenasmall: push refused: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    // Reserving storage and USING it are two claims, and the second is the one
    // that says the address the arena handed back is inside the region the
    // linker reserved rather than merely a number.
    if within[0] != WITNESS {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS arenasmall: witness LOST\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS arenasmall: 2 KiB ok\n")

    // BRACKET TWO. Printed first, because the ask below does not return: the
    // next line in the transcript is the runtime's, not this program's.
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS arenasmall: asking 16 KiB\n")
    let beyond = try Vector<UInt32>(capacity: BEYOND_WORDS) catch {
        // Unreachable on this runtime — see the header. If it is ever reached,
        // the allocator grew an error channel and this case should be rewritten
        // to assert THAT, which is why the arm says so rather than passing.
        print("SOS arenasmall: 16 KiB refused as a STATUS: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // Unreachable on a correct build: the ask above stopped the machine. A run
    // that gets here linked the shared 64 KiB arena and not this package's, so
    // it exits non-zero rather than shutting down clean.
    print("SOS arenasmall: UNREACHABLE, got {} words\n", beyond.len())
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}
